#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr ""
"Isang libreng kurso ng IBM tungkol sa quantum information at computation"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: sidebar_label
#, no-wrap
msgid "The query model of computation"
msgstr "Ang query model ng computation"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When we model computations in mathematical terms, we typically have in mind "
"the sort of process represented by the following figure, where information "
"is provided as input, a computation takes place, and output is produced.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kapag nag-momodel tayo ng mga computation sa mathematical na paraan, "
"karaniwang iniisip natin ang uri ng proseso na ipinapakita sa sumusunod na "
"figure, kung saan ang impormasyon ay ibinibigay bilang input, nagaganap ang "
"isang computation, at nalilikha ang output.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Illustration of a standard "
"computation.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/standard-computation.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Ilustrasyon ng isang karaniwang "
"computation.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/standard-computation.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"While it is true that the computers we use today continuously receive input and produce output, essentially interacting both with us and with other computers in a way not reflected by the figure, the intention is not to represent the ongoing operation of computers.\n"
"Rather, it is to create a simple abstraction of computation, focusing on isolated computational tasks.\n"
"For example, the input might encode a number, a vector, a matrix, a graph, a description of a molecule, or something more complicated, while the output encodes a solution to the computational task we have in mind.\n"
msgstr ""
"Bagama't totoo na ang mga computer na ginagamit natin ngayon ay patuloy na tumatanggap ng input at gumagawa ng output — na mahalagang nakikipag-ugnayan sa atin at sa ibang mga computer sa paraang hindi narerepresenta ng figure — ang layunin ay hindi ilarawan ang patuloy na operasyon ng mga computer.\n"
"Sa halip, ito ay lumikha ng isang simpleng abstraction ng computation, na nakatuon sa mga naka-isolate na computational task.\n"
"Halimbawa, ang input ay maaaring mag-encode ng isang numero, vector, matrix, graph, paglalarawan ng isang molekula, o isang bagay na mas kumplikado, habang ang output ay nag-e-encode ng solusyon sa computational task na iniisip natin.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The key point is that the input is provided to the computation, usually in "
"the form of a binary string, with no part of it being hidden.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang pangunahing punto ay ang input ay ibinibigay sa computation, karaniwang "
"sa anyo ng isang binary string, nang walang anumang bahagi nito na "
"nakatago.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Description of the model {#description-of-the-model}"
msgstr "Paglalarawan ng modelo {#description-of-the-model}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the *query model* of computation, the entire input is not provided to the computation like in a more standard model suggested above.\n"
"Rather, the input is made available in the form of a *function*, which the computation accesses by making *queries*.\n"
"Alternatively, we may view computations in the query model as having\n"
"<DefinitionTooltip definition=\"Random access, also known as direct access, refers to the ability to look at elements at chosen positions within a sequence directly, without having to scan through the entire sequence to find them (which is called sequential access).\">random access</DefinitionTooltip>\n"
"to bits (or segments of bits) of the input.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa *query model* ng computation, ang buong input ay hindi ibinibigay sa computation tulad ng sa mas karaniwang modelo na iminungkahi sa itaas.\n"
"Sa halip, ang input ay ginagawang available sa anyo ng isang *function*, na ina-access ng computation sa pamamagitan ng paggawa ng mga *query*.\n"
"Bilang alternatibo, maaari nating tingnan ang mga computation sa query model bilang may\n"
"<DefinitionTooltip definition=\"Random access, also known as direct access, refers to the ability to look at elements at chosen positions within a sequence directly, without having to scan through the entire sequence to find them (which is called sequential access).\">random access</DefinitionTooltip>\n"
"sa mga bit (o mga segment ng bit) ng input.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Illustration of a computation in the query "
"model.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/quantum-"
"query-algorithms/query-computation.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Ilustrasyon ng isang computation sa query "
"model.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/quantum-"
"query-algorithms/query-computation.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We often refer to the input as being provided by an *oracle* or *black box* in the context of the query model.\n"
"Both terms suggest that a complete description of the input is hidden from the computation, with the only way to access it being to ask questions.\n"
"It is as if we're consulting the Oracle at Delphi about the input: she won't tell us everything she knows, she only answers specific questions.\n"
"The term *black box* makes sense especially when we think about the input as being represented by a function;\n"
"we cannot look inside the function and understand how it works, we can only evaluate it on arguments we select.\n"
msgstr ""
"Madalas nating tinutukoy ang input bilang ibinibigay ng isang *oracle* o *black box* sa konteksto ng query model.\n"
"Ang parehong termino ay nagmumungkahi na ang kumpletong paglalarawan ng input ay nakatago mula sa computation, at ang tanging paraan upang ma-access ito ay ang magtanong.\n"
"Para bang kumukonsulta tayo sa Oracle sa Delphi tungkol sa input: hindi niya sasabihin sa atin ang lahat ng alam niya, sumasagot lang siya sa mga tiyak na tanong.\n"
"Ang terminong *black box* ay may katuturan lalo na kapag iniisip natin ang input bilang kinakatawan ng isang function;\n"
"hindi natin maaaring tingnan ang loob ng function at maunawaan kung paano ito gumagana, maaari lang nating e-evaluate ito sa mga argumento na aming pinipili.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We're going to be working exclusively with binary strings in this lesson, as opposed to strings containing different symbols, so let's write $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ hereafter to refer to the binary alphabet for convenience.\n"
"We'll be thinking about different computational problems, with some simple examples described shortly, but for all of them the input will be represented by a function taking the form\n"
msgstr ""
"Gagamit tayo ng eksklusibong mga binary string sa araling ito, sa halip na mga string na naglalaman ng iba't ibang simbolo, kaya isulatin natin ang $\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}$ mula rito upang tumukoy sa binary alphabet para sa kaginhawaan.\n"
"Mag-iisip tayo ng iba't ibang computational problem, na may ilang simpleng halimbawa na inilalarawan sa lalong madaling panahon, ngunit para sa lahat ng ito ang input ay kinakatawan ng isang function na may sumusunod na anyo\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for two positive integers $n$ and $m.$\n"
"Naturally, we could choose a different name in place of $f,$ but we'll stick with $f$ throughout the lesson.\n"
msgstr ""
"para sa dalawang positibong integer na $n$ at $m.$\n"
"Natural, maaari tayong pumili ng ibang pangalan sa halip na $f,$ ngunit mananatili tayo sa $f$ sa buong aralin.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To say that a computation makes a *query* means that some string $x \\in \\Sigma^n$ is selected, and then the string $f(x)\\in\\Sigma^m$ is made available to the computation by the oracle.\n"
"The precise way that this works for quantum algorithms will be discussed shortly — we need to make sure that this is possible to do with a unitary quantum operation allowing queries to be made in superposition — but for now we can think about it intuitively at a high level.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang sabihing ang isang computation ay gumagawa ng *query* ay nangangahulugang may napiling string na $x \\in \\Sigma^n,$ at pagkatapos ay ginagawang available ang string na $f(x)\\in\\Sigma^m$ sa computation ng oracle.\n"
"Ang tiyak na paraan na ito ay gumagana para sa mga quantum algorithm ay tatalakayin sa lalong madaling panahon — kailangan nating tiyakin na posible itong gawin gamit ang isang unitary quantum operation na nagpapahintulot na gumawa ng mga query sa superposition — ngunit sa ngayon maaari nating isipin ito sa mataas na antas nang intuitive.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, the way that we'll measure efficiency of query algorithms is simple: we'll count the *number of queries* they require.\n"
"This is related to the time required to perform a computation, but it's not exactly the same because we're ignoring the time for operations other than the queries, and we're also treating the queries as if they each have unit cost.\n"
"We can take the operations besides the queries into account if we wish (and this is sometimes done), but restricting our attention just to the number of queries helps to keep things simple.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa wakas, ang paraan na susukat tayo ng kahusayan ng mga query algorithm ay simple: bibilangin natin ang *bilang ng mga query* na kinakailangan nila.\n"
"Ito ay may kaugnayan sa oras na kinakailangan upang magsagawa ng isang computation, ngunit hindi ito eksaktong katulad dahil binabalewala natin ang oras para sa mga operasyon maliban sa mga query, at tinatrato rin natin ang mga query na parang bawat isa ay may unit cost.\n"
"Maaari nating isaalang-alang ang mga operasyon bukod sa mga query kung gusto natin (at minsan ginagawa ito), ngunit ang pagtutok lamang sa bilang ng mga query ay nakakatulong na panatilihing simple ang mga bagay.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples of query problems {#examples-of-query-problems}"
msgstr "Mga halimbawa ng query problem {#examples-of-query-problems}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here are a few simple examples of query problems.\n"
msgstr "Narito ang ilang simpleng halimbawa ng mga query problem.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**OR.** The input function takes the form $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma$"
" (so $m=1$ for this problem). The task is to output $1$ if there exists a "
"string $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ for which $f(x) = 1,$ and to output $0$ if there is "
"no such string. If we think about the function $f$ as representing a "
"sequence of $2^n$ bits to which we have random access, the problem is to "
"compute the OR of these bits.\n"
msgstr ""
"**OR.** Ang input function ay may anyo na $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma$"
" (kaya $m=1$ para sa problemang ito). Ang gawain ay mag-output ng $1$ kung "
"mayroong string na $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ kung saan $f(x) = 1,$ at mag-output ng "
"$0$ kung walang ganitong string. Kung iniisip natin ang function na $f$ "
"bilang kumakatawan sa isang sequence ng $2^n$ na bit na may random access "
"tayo, ang problema ay ang i-compute ang OR ng mga bit na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- **Parity.** The input function again takes the form $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma.$ The task is to determine whether the number of strings $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ for which $f(x) = 1$ is *even* or *odd*.\n"
"To be precise, the required output is $0$ if the set $\\{x\\in\\Sigma^n : f(x) = 1\\}$ has an even number of elements and $1$ if it has an odd number of elements. If we think about the function $f$ as representing a sequence of $2^n$ bits to which we have random access, the problem is to compute the parity (or exclusive-OR) of these bits.\n"
msgstr ""
"- **Parity.** Ang input function ay muli may anyo na $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma.$ Ang gawain ay tukuyin kung ang bilang ng mga string na $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ kung saan $f(x) = 1$ ay *even* o *odd*.\n"
"Upang maging tiyak, ang kinakailangang output ay $0$ kung ang set na $\\{x\\in\\Sigma^n : f(x) = 1\\}$ ay may even na bilang ng mga elemento at $1$ kung ito ay may odd na bilang ng mga elemento. Kung iniisip natin ang function na $f$ bilang kumakatawan sa isang sequence ng $2^n$ na bit na may random access tayo, ang problema ay ang i-compute ang parity (o exclusive-OR) ng mga bit na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- **Minimum.** The input function takes the form $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m$ for any choices of positive integers $n$ and $m.$ The required output is the string $y \\in \\{f(x) : x\\in\\Sigma^n\\}$ that comes first in the lexicographic (that is, dictionary) ordering of $\\Sigma^m.$\n"
"If we think about the function $f$ as representing a sequence of $2^n$ integers encoded as strings of length $m$ in binary notation to which we have random access, the problem is to compute the minimum of these integers.\n"
msgstr ""
"- **Minimum.** Ang input function ay may anyo na $f:\\Sigma^n \\rightarrow \\Sigma^m$ para sa anumang pagpipilian ng mga positibong integer na $n$ at $m.$ Ang kinakailangang output ay ang string na $y \\in \\{f(x) : x\\in\\Sigma^n\\}$ na unang lumabas sa lexicographic (iyon ay, dictionary) na pagkakasunud-sunod ng $\\Sigma^m.$\n"
"Kung iniisip natin ang function na $f$ bilang kumakatawan sa isang sequence ng $2^n$ na integer na naka-encode bilang mga string ng haba na $m$ sa binary notation na may random access tayo, ang problema ay ang i-compute ang minimum ng mga integer na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We also consider query problems where we have a *promise* on the input.\n"
"What this means is that we're given some sort of guarantee on the input, and we're not responsible for what happens when this guarantee is not met.\n"
"Another way to describe this type of problem is to say that some input functions (the ones for which the promise is not satisfied) are considered as \"don't care\" inputs.\n"
"No requirements at all are placed on algorithms when they're given \"don't care\" inputs.\n"
msgstr ""
"Isinasaalang-alang din natin ang mga query problem kung saan mayroon tayong *promise* sa input.\n"
"Ang ibig sabihin nito ay binibigyan tayo ng ilang uri ng garantiya sa input, at hindi tayo responsable para sa mangyayari kapag ang garantiyang ito ay hindi natutupad.\n"
"Isa pang paraan upang ilarawan ang ganitong uri ng problema ay sabihin na ang ilang input function (ang mga kung saan hindi nasatisfied ang promise) ay itinuturing na \"don't care\" na mga input.\n"
"Walang anumang mga kinakailangan ang inilalagay sa mga algorithm kapag binibigyan sila ng \"don't care\" na mga input.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here's one example of a problem with a promise:\n"
msgstr "Narito ang isang halimbawa ng isang problema na may promise:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Unique search.** The input function takes the form $f:\\Sigma^n "
"\\rightarrow \\Sigma,$ and we are *promised* that there is exactly one "
"string $z\\in\\Sigma^n$ for which $f(z) = 1,$ with $f(x) = 0$ for all "
"strings $x\\neq z.$ The task is to find this unique string $z.$\n"
msgstr ""
"**Unique search.** Ang input function ay may anyo na $f:\\Sigma^n "
"\\rightarrow \\Sigma,$ at *ipinangako* na mayroon lamang isang string na "
"$z\\in\\Sigma^n$ kung saan $f(z) = 1,$ na may $f(x) = 0$ para sa lahat ng "
"string na $x\\neq z.$ Ang gawain ay hanapin ang natatanging string na $z.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All four of the examples just described are natural, in the sense that "
"they're easy to describe and we can imagine a variety of situations or "
"contexts in which they might arise.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang lahat ng apat na halimbawang nabanggit ay natural, sa kahulugang madali "
"itong ilarawan at maaari nating isipin ang iba't ibang sitwasyon o konteksto"
" kung saan maaari itong lumabas.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In contrast, some query problems aren't \"natural\" like this at all.\n"
"In fact, in the study of the query model, we sometimes come up with very complicated and highly contrived problems where it's difficult to imagine that anyone would ever actually want to solve them in practice.\n"
"This doesn't mean that the problems aren't interesting, though!\n"
"Things that might seem contrived or unnatural at first can provide unexpected clues or inspire new ideas.\n"
"Shor's quantum algorithm for factoring, which was inspired by Simon's algorithm, is a great example.\n"
"It's also an important part of the study of the query model to look for extremes, which can shed light on both the potential advantages and the limitations of quantum computing.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa kabaligtaran, ang ilang mga query problem ay hindi \"natural\" tulad nito.\n"
"Sa katunayan, sa pag-aaral ng query model, minsan ay lumilikha tayo ng napakakomplikado at lubhang gawa-gawa na mga problema kung saan mahirap isipin na may sinuman ay talagang gustong lutasin ang mga ito sa practice.\n"
"Hindi ito nangangahulugang ang mga problema ay hindi kawili-wili, bagaman!\n"
"Ang mga bagay na maaaring mukhang gawa-gawa o hindi natural sa simula ay maaaring magbigay ng mga hindi inaasahang pahiwatig o magbigay inspirasyon ng mga bagong ideya.\n"
"Ang quantum algorithm ni Shor para sa factoring, na inspirado ng algorithm ni Simon, ay isang magandang halimbawa.\n"
"Ito rin ay isang mahalagang bahagi ng pag-aaral ng query model na maghanap ng mga sukdulan, na maaaring magbigay-liwanag sa parehong mga potensyal na kalamangan at limitasyon ng quantum computing.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Query gates {#query-gates}"
msgstr "Mga query gate {#query-gates}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When we're describing computations with circuits, queries are made by "
"special gates called *query gates*.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kapag naglalarawan tayo ng mga computation gamit ang mga circuit, ginagawa "
"ang mga query sa pamamagitan ng mga espesyal na gate na tinatawag na *query "
"gate*.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The simplest way to define query gates for classical Boolean circuits is to "
"simply allow them to compute the input function $f$ directly, as the "
"following figure suggests.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang pinakasimpleng paraan upang tukuyin ang mga query gate para sa mga "
"classical Boolean circuit ay ang payagan silang direktang i-compute ang "
"input function na $f,$ tulad ng iminumungkahi ng sumusunod na figure.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A classical query gate.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/classical-query-gate.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Isang classical query gate.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-"
"quantum-algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/classical-query-gate.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When a Boolean circuit is created for a query problem, the input function $f$ is accessed through these gates, and the number of queries that the circuit makes is simply the number of query gates that appear in the circuit.\n"
"The input wires of the Boolean circuit itself are initialized to fixed values, which should be considered as part of the algorithm (as opposed to being inputs to the problem).\n"
msgstr ""
"Kapag ang isang Boolean circuit ay nilikha para sa isang query problem, ang input function na $f$ ay ina-access sa pamamagitan ng mga gate na ito, at ang bilang ng mga query na ginagawa ng circuit ay simpleng bilang ng mga query gate na lumalabas sa circuit.\n"
"Ang mga input wire ng Boolean circuit mismo ay ini-initialize sa mga fixed na halaga, na dapat ituring bilang bahagi ng algorithm (kumpara sa pagiging mga input sa problema).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, here's a Boolean circuit with classical query gates that solves"
" the parity problem described above for a function of the form "
"$f:\\Sigma\\rightarrow\\Sigma$:\n"
msgstr ""
"Halimbawa, narito ang isang Boolean circuit na may mga classical query gate "
"na naglulutas ng parity problem na inilarawan sa itaas para sa isang "
"function ng anyo na $f:\\Sigma\\rightarrow\\Sigma$:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Classical query algorithm for "
"parity.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/classical-parity.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Classical query algorithm para sa "
"parity.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/classical-parity.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This algorithm makes two queries because there are two query gates.\n"
"The way it works is that the function $f$ is queried on the two possible inputs, $0$ and $1,$ and the results are plugged into a Boolean circuit that computes the XOR.\n"
"(This particular circuit appeared as an example of a Boolean circuit in the *Quantum circuits* lesson of the *Basics of quantum information* course.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang algorithm na ito ay gumagawa ng dalawang query dahil may dalawang query gate.\n"
"Ang paraan ng pagtatrabaho nito ay ang function na $f$ ay ique-query sa dalawang posibleng input, $0$ at $1,$ at ang mga resulta ay ipinasok sa isang Boolean circuit na nag-co-compute ng XOR.\n"
"(Ang partikular na circuit na ito ay lumabas bilang isang halimbawa ng Boolean circuit sa aralin na *Quantum circuits* ng kursong *Basics of quantum information*.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For quantum circuits, this definition of query gates doesn't work, because these gates will be non-unitary for some choices of the function $f.$\n"
"So, what we do instead is to define *unitary query gates* that operate as this figure suggests on standard basis states:\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa mga quantum circuit, ang kahulugang ito ng mga query gate ay hindi gumagana, dahil ang mga gate na ito ay magiging non-unitary para sa ilang pagpipilian ng function na $f.$\n"
"Kaya, ang gagawin natin sa halip ay tukuyin ang mga *unitary query gate* na gumaganap tulad ng iminumungkahi ng figure na ito sa mga standard basis state:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A unitary query gate.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-"
"algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/unitary-query-gate.svg)\n"
msgstr ""
"![Isang unitary query gate.](/learning/images/courses/fundamentals-of-"
"quantum-algorithms/quantum-query-algorithms/unitary-query-gate.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, our assumption is that $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ and $y\\in\\Sigma^m$ are arbitrary strings.\n"
"The notation $y\\oplus f(x)$ refers to the *bitwise exclusive-OR* of two strings, which have length $m$ in this case.\n"
"For example, $001 \\oplus 101 = 100.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Dito, ang ating pagpapalagay ay ang $x\\in\\Sigma^n$ at $y\\in\\Sigma^m$ ay mga arbitrary na string.\n"
"Ang notasyong $y\\oplus f(x)$ ay tumutukoy sa *bitwise exclusive-OR* ng dalawang string, na may haba na $m$ sa kasong ito.\n"
"Halimbawa, $001 \\oplus 101 = 100.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Intuitively speaking, what the gate $U_f$ does (for any chosen function $f$) is to echo the top input string $x$ and XOR the function value $f(x)$ onto the bottom input string $y,$ which is a unitary operation for every choice for the function $f.$\n"
"In fact, it's a deterministic operation, and it is its own inverse.\n"
"This implies that, as a matrix, $U_f$ is always a *permutation matrix*, meaning a matrix with a single $1$ in each row and each column, with all other entries being $0.$\n"
"Applying a permutation matrix to a vector simply shuffles the entries of the vector (hence the term *permutation matrix*), and therefore does not change that vector's Euclidean norm — revealing that permutation matrices are always unitary.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa intuitive na pagsasalita, ang ginagawa ng gate na $U_f$ (para sa anumang piniling function na $f$) ay ang i-echo ang itaas na input string na $x$ at i-XOR ang function value na $f(x)$ sa ibabang input string na $y,$ na isang unitary operation para sa bawat pagpipilian ng function na $f.$\n"
"Sa katunayan, ito ay isang deterministic na operasyon, at ito ang sarili nitong inverse.\n"
"Nangangahulugan ito na, bilang isang matrix, ang $U_f$ ay palaging isang *permutation matrix*, ibig sabihin isang matrix na may iisang $1$ sa bawat row at bawat column, na ang lahat ng iba pang entry ay $0.$\n"
"Ang pag-apply ng permutation matrix sa isang vector ay simpleng nag-s-shuffle ng mga entry ng vector (kaya ang terminong *permutation matrix*), at samakatuwid ay hindi binabago ang Euclidean norm ng vector na iyon — nagpapakita na ang mga permutation matrix ay palaging unitary.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It should be highlighted that, when we analyze query algorithms by simply counting the number of queries that a query algorithm makes, we're completely ignoring the difficulty of physically constructing the query gates — for both the classical and quantum versions just described.\n"
"Intuitively speaking, the construction of the query gates is part of the preparation of the input, not part of finding a solution.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dapat i-highlight na, kapag sinusuri natin ang mga query algorithm sa pamamagitan lamang ng pagbibilang ng bilang ng mga query na ginagawa ng isang query algorithm, ganap nating binabalewala ang kahirapan ng pisikal na pagtatayo ng mga query gate — para sa parehong classical at quantum na mga bersyon na nabanggit kanina.\n"
"Sa intuitive na pagsasalita, ang konstruksyon ng mga query gate ay bahagi ng paghahanda ng input, hindi bahagi ng paghahanap ng solusyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That might seem unreasonable, but we must keep in mind that we're not trying to describe practical computing or fully account for the resources required.\n"
"Rather, we're defining a theoretical model that helps to shed light on the potential advantages of quantum computing.\n"
"We'll have more to say about this point in the lesson following this one when we turn our attention to a more standard model of computation where inputs are given explicitly to circuits as binary strings.\n"
msgstr ""
"Maaaring mukhang hindi makatuwiran iyon, ngunit kailangan nating tandaan na hindi tayo nagtatangkang ilarawan ang praktikal na computing o ganap na i-account ang mga kinakailangang resources.\n"
"Sa halip, nagtatukoy tayo ng isang theoretical model na tumutulong na magbigay-liwanag sa mga potensyal na kalamangan ng quantum computing.\n"
"Magkakaroon tayo ng mas maraming sasabihin tungkol sa puntong ito sa aralin kasunod nito kapag ibinalik natin ang ating atensyon sa isang mas karaniwang modelo ng computation kung saan ang mga input ay ibinibigay nang tahasan sa mga circuit bilang mga binary string.\n"
